An automotive turbocharger is a forced induction device used to increase the power output of an internal combustion engine by compressing air. Under the Harmonized System, turbochargers are considered parts of internal combustion engines. They are specifically classified under heading 8414, which covers air pumps, vacuum pumps, and compressors. Within this heading, subheadings differentiate between various types (e.g., vacuum pumps, hand- or foot-operated air pumps, compressors for refrigerating equipment). Turbochargers are classified as "Turbochargers" under subheading 8414.59. 

While these are used for automotive engines, the HS nomenclature places them under 8414 rather than Chapter 87 (Vehicles) because they are considered parts of general-purpose machinery/engines, and they do not lose their identity as compressors. The classification is well-settled at the 6-digit level.
</ANALYSIS>

