An automotive turbocharger is a forced induction device used to increase the efficiency and power output of an internal combustion engine by compressing air. Under the Harmonized System, turbochargers are considered parts of internal combustion engines. 

They fall under Chapter 84 (Nuclear reactors, boilers, machinery and mechanical appliances; parts thereof). Specifically, Heading 8414 covers air or vacuum pumps, air or other gas compressors, and fans. While a turbocharger is a type of compressor, the Explanatory Notes to the Harmonized System explicitly categorize turbochargers (exhaust gas turbo-blowers) for internal combustion engines under Heading 8414.

